# In a manner as if wholly [[engrossed]] or engaged. {{defdate|First attested in the mid 19th century.}}<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=9}}</ref>
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1841|author={{w|Richard Brinsley Peake|R. B. Peake}}|title=The Bequeathed Heart|journal=w:The New Monthly Magazine|section=Volume 62, 1841 part 2, p. 36|url=https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/010307785
|passage=Michael O’Shea continued '''absorbedly''' gazing on the picture, till the welcome smell of a tumbler of Mr. Hoolagan’s whisky suddenly restored him to comparative serenity.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1917|author=w:Edna Ferber|title=Fanny Herself|location=New York|publisher=Grosset & Dunlap|pages=65–66|url=https://archive.org/details/fannyherselfbyed00ferbrich
|passage=Fanny, red-cheeked and bright-eyed from her recent mental struggles, listened interestedly, then intently, then '''absorbedly'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1943|author={{w|Mary Norton (author)|Mary Norton}}|title=The Magic Bed-Knob|location=New York|publisher=Hyperion|chapter=8|url=https://archive.org/details/magicbedknoborho00nort
|passage=Carey could see her expression. It was the face of one who was deeply and '''absorbedly''' interested.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2010|author=w:Howard Jacobson|title=w:The Finkler Question|location=New York|publisher=Bloomsbury|section=Part One, Chapter Four, I, p. 86|url=https://archive.org/details/finklerquestion00bloo
|passage=Treslove was no Finkler. He could not lose his heart to more than one woman at a time. He loved too '''absorbedly''' for that. But he always knew when he was about to be thrown over and was quick to make provision, where he could, to love '''absorbedly''' again.}}

